WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.042
Blogging in PageMotor.

00:00:02.042 --> 00:00:07.297
There is no blog button, and that surprises people arriving from WordPress.

00:00:07.297 --> 00:00:12.203
The surprise runs the other way too: most of a blog was already there.

00:00:13.603 --> 00:00:16.837
A blog is really four things wearing one name.

00:00:16.837 --> 00:00:22.322
A place to write, with dates and authors. A front page for them, newest first.

00:00:22.322 --> 00:00:27.314
A way to group them, categories and tags. And the plumbing around them:

00:00:27.314 --> 00:00:29.845
feeds, comments, scheduling, search.

00:00:29.845 --> 00:00:35.259
PageMotor core covers the first almost completely. It half-covers the second.

00:00:35.259 --> 00:00:38.634
And the plumbing is ordinary EP Suite territory.

00:00:40.034 --> 00:00:42.700
So there is exactly one honest gap.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:52.071
Core stores every piece of content with a publish date, a modified date and an author, and every page nests under a parent.

00:00:52.071 --> 00:00:54.967
The data model is blog-shaped already.

00:00:54.967 --> 00:01:00.528
What it does not do is loop over that data. There is no screen that says:

00:01:00.528 --> 00:01:05.785
list everything under Journal, newest first, with an excerpt of each.

00:01:05.785 --> 00:01:08.223
That missing organ is the index.

00:01:09.623 --> 00:01:15.853
Which means you can run a perfectly respectable blog today with nothing but pages.

00:01:15.853 --> 00:01:17.904
Make a page called Journal.

00:01:17.904 --> 00:01:24.589
Write each post as its own page with Journal as its parent, so the URL nests underneath.

00:01:24.589 --> 00:01:30.591
Then curate the Journal page by hand: a heading, a line about the post, a link.

00:01:30.591 --> 00:01:32.490
That is not a workaround.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:37.580
For a small, considered publication it is arguably the better tool.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:42.051
The hand-built index is a feature in disguise.

00:01:42.051 --> 00:01:48.327
You choose the order, so your best work sits on top forever instead of sliding off page three.

00:01:48.327 --> 00:01:52.400
You write the blurbs, so they read like an editor wrote them.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.870
And there are no moving parts at all.

00:01:54.870 --> 00:02:05.218
It starts to hurt when you publish often, because every post is two edits, and past twenty or thirty pieces the hand-kept index becomes the chore you skip.

00:02:06.618 --> 00:02:14.104
Four plugins cover what most people actually mean by blog features, and none of them needs EP Blog.

00:02:14.104 --> 00:02:15.843
EP RSS serves the feed.

00:02:15.843 --> 00:02:20.229
EP Comments puts the conversation where the writing lives.

00:02:20.229 --> 00:02:24.464
EP Scheduled Content publishes a draft on a future date.

00:02:24.464 --> 00:02:30.135
And EP Search becomes the archive key once the archive outgrows one screen.

00:02:30.135 --> 00:02:38.755
Each works identically with plain pages and with posts, so nothing you set up is wasted if you change route later.

00:02:40.155 --> 00:02:42.343
EP Blog adds the missing organ.

00:02:42.343 --> 00:02:51.517
Drop its shortcode on a page and it renders every live post, newest first, with excerpts, category badges, bylines and pagination.

00:02:51.517 --> 00:02:54.552
Publishing becomes one edit instead of two.

00:02:54.552 --> 00:03:08.808
You also get nested categories and tags, structured data on every post, and optional category archives that are real PageMotor pages, so each one carries its own title, meta description and social card.

00:03:10.208 --> 00:03:11.830
So the choice is simple.

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:21.560
If you publish a few deliberate pieces a year and want to curate how they are presented, plain pages plus a feed is a complete and elegant blog.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:28.723
The moment publishing becomes a habit rather than an event, install EP Blog and let the index keep itself.

00:03:28.723 --> 00:03:31.967
Nothing from the pages route is lost either way:

00:03:31.967 --> 00:03:34.264
the two live happily side by side.

00:03:35.664 --> 00:03:41.098
The guide has both routes side by side, row by row, and the docs for every plugin mentioned.
